Z||000000||AkursedX||04-27-2001||01:22 AM||||Hello.  Well I've finally got my rear decklid painted, and it'll be on my car tomorrow, which means my car will FINALLY be on the road!!  <BR>And the 1st place I'm taking it to is the alignment shop  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <BR>See, I have installed the Fierostore's sport springs which gave me a 1 inch drop.  (It's an 88GT by the way)  So needless to say, my wheels are really bent in right now.  <BR>Now I know they probably won't be able to get my front camber perfect because it'll run out of adjustment, but that's ok.  But they should be able to get my rear in alignment properly right?  I had a post on this a while ago and someone said they might need to grind some material out in the adjustment slot in the strut.  <P>Soooo.....finally, my question is:  Will the alignment shop grind out this slot for me or should I take out my strut again (Newly installed KYB's), and grind it out myself?<P>Any input is appreciated  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Gee, I guess that wasn't really a quick question, lol||||reg||5
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-27-2001||01:59 PM||||I would let the alignment shop have a crack at it. There is considerable adjustment in the strut knuckles. That is if the shop isn't easily scared off. <P>For a lark I took out my front sway bar and painted it red just because I was doing urethane and it looked terrible and I had the can. Months later I took it to get realigned and the shop actually REFUSED to align it with modified suspension. I had to call them up and tell them it wasn't even performance paint that I used on the stock sway bar (everything else was stock)! ||||reg||6
Z||000002||gixxer||04-27-2001||05:47 PM||||On my '88, I adjusted the struts into the max negative camber position before I had it aligned, thereby verifying that the alignment guy had enough adjustment to work with. If you have a visible negative camber to the suspension, it'll be enough.<BR>Was able to tweak the Konis to -1.5*.||||reg||1
